# =============================================================================
# device-summa-filum — SFR-6 distributed-sum proof kernel
# (summa-filum-reduction)
# =============================================================================
#
# What this proves:
#   • The declared distributed sum (`summa ex … filum …`, SFR-5 plan entry)
#     lowers through the Metal native collective arm (SFR-6): cyclic
#     per-lane term-body inline with private register accumulation, exactly
#     one simd_sum at group exit, broadcast result — via the SAME ordinary
#     command:
#
#       faber run --backend metal <package>    # Apple M5 Max (burgus)
#
#   • The kernel is an F16 column-dot: every input value, the F16-grid
#     weight, and every per-term product sit exactly on the binary16 grid,
#     so the f32 lane accumulations are exact; the comparison against the
#     sequential-spelling oracle still runs under the numeric-policy
#     tolerance contract (reduction-sum row) — never a bit-exact assertion.
#
# The host input values live in faber.toml [device] inputs (pinned with the
# oracle); the CPU reference is pinned in oracle/ (numeric-policy v1.0.0,
# reduction-sum row: atol=1e-6, rtol=1e-6).
#
# See also: summa, filum, nucleum
# =============================================================================

@ nucleum
functio column_dot(tf32[32] a, tf32[1] out, u32 id) → vacuum {
    fixum f32 total ← summa ex a apud [i] filum f fixum s {
        varia f32 t ← s * 1.4140625
        redde t
    }
    out[id] ← total
}
